This folder contains scripts used to conduct the simulation study presented in the
manuscript. The simulations explore the performance of the spatially explicit integrated
population model (sIPM).

Main simulation study

- Perfect_alignment.R: Runs simulations under the scenario where count and
  capture-recapture data are perfectly aligned in space and time.
- Spatial_misalignment.R: Runs simulations where count and capture-recapture data are
  spatially misaligned, without temporal misalignment.
- SC_Spatio_temporal_misalignment_high.R: Simulations with spatial misalignment and high
  temporal misalignment, using a spatially constant covariate effect.
- SC_Spatio_temporal_misalignment_low.R: Simulations with spatial misalignment and low
  temporal misalignment, using a spatially constant covariate effect.
- SV_Spatio_temporal_misalignment_high.R: Simulations with spatial misalignment and high
  temporal misalignment, using a spatially varying covariate effect.
- SV_Spatio_temporal_misalignment_low.R: Simulations with spatial misalignment and low
  temporal misalignment, using a spatially varying covariate effect.

Sampling_intensity/

Contains the additional experiment reported in Section S1.6 of the Supporting Information,
which repeats the spatial misalignment scenario at four levels of sampling intensity
(M = 80, 200, 500 and 800 locations used for model fitting). See the README in that folder
for design details, run instructions and computation times.

Recommended Execution Order

1. Perfect_alignment.R - establishes baseline performance.
2. Spatial_misalignment.R - explores purely spatial misalignment.
3. SC / SV scripts - explore combinations of spatial and temporal misalignment with
   different covariate specifications.
4. Sampling_intensity/ - explores the effect of the number of sampling locations.

Notes

All scripts assume dependencies in packages_needed.R have been installed. Expected
computation times for each script are reported in Section S1.3 and Table S8 of the
Supporting Information.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE ON MCMC SETTINGS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each script uses the final 1000 posterior samples per chain for prediction,
selected by explicit row indices. Two MCMC configurations are used across the
simulation study, and the indices differ accordingly:

  Scripts                              runMCMC settings                  Indices
  ----------------------------------   -------------------------------   -----------------
  Perfect_alignment.R                  niter    = 85000                  primary:   1501:2500
  Spatial_misalignment.R               nburnin  = 60000                  secondary:  251:1250
  SC_Spatio_temporal_misalignment_     thin     = 10
    high.R and _low.R                  thin2    = 20
  Sampling intensity/M80.R, M200.R,
    M500.R and M800.R

  SV_Spatio_temporal_misalignment_     niter    = 125000                 primary:   2001:3000
    high.R and _low.R                  nburnin  = 80000                  secondary:  801:1800
                                       thin     = 15
                                       thin2    = 25

The first configuration retains (85000 - 60000)/10 = 2500 primary and
(85000 - 60000)/20 = 1250 secondary samples per chain; the second retains
45000/15 = 3000 and 45000/25 = 1800. In each case the indices select the last
1000 rows.

The spatially varying scripts use longer chains because the additional regression
coefficients mix more slowly.

IMPORTANT: if you change niter, nburnin, thin or thin2, these row indices will no
longer be valid. R returns NA rather than an error for out-of-range row indices,
so the scripts will run to completion but produce NA or invalid predictions with
no warning. If you alter the MCMC settings, update both index ranges to select the
final 1000 rows of each chain.
